Title: The fixture README's per-record table has no test cross-checking it against recipe.json
Created: 2026-09-03
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-09-03T15:46Z Minh Ha-Duong created

--- body ---
## Context

PR #298 (recovering an interrupted Codex session's ticket-0029 work) replayed
a commit that rewrote `bench/fixtures/README.md`'s "What the recipe holds"
paragraph and its per-record table back to stale, pre-HAL-unpin content: the
four HAL rows' "bytes pinned" column read `sha256` with fabricated page
counts (256, 7, 2) while the live `recipe.json` had `sha256: null` and
`page_count: null` for all four. `test_live_recipe_tally_is_swept_into_its_documentation`
(`tests/test_fixture_recipe.py`) is the test that exists specifically to
catch this class of drift, and it passed anyway — because it only
regex-matches the prose paragraph's aggregate counts, never the per-row
table. A first "repair" commit on the PR (`a95f089`) fixed the prose and
still missed the table for the same reason; the actual fix landed only after
`/gaze` review caught it by hand-diffing the table against `recipe.json`.

This is exactly the class `AGENTS.md` names as the project's "most expensive
recurring defect" — duplicated numbers drifting apart — just one level down:
not a duplicated *aggregate*, but a duplicated *per-row* fact (each record's
hash-pinned status and page count), with the existing test covering only the
aggregate.

## Relevant files

- `tests/test_fixture_recipe.py` — `test_live_recipe_tally_is_swept_into_its_documentation`
  owns this cross-check today, aggregate-only.
- `bench/fixtures/README.md` — the "## What the recipe holds" table, one row
  per `recipe.json` entry, columns include `pages` and `bytes pinned`.
- `bench/fixtures/recipe.json` — the source of truth each row should mirror
  (`page_count`, `sha256`).

## Actions

1. Extend (or add alongside) the existing tally test: for every row in the
   README table, parse its `id`, `pages`, and `bytes pinned` cell and assert
   they match the corresponding `recipe.json` entry's `page_count` and
   hash-pinned state (`sha256` present vs the table's open-state label).
2. Decide how to represent `page_count: null` in the table (currently `—`)
   and enforce that mapping too, so a fabricated page count is caught the
   same way a wrong pin-status is.

## Test

Red step: revert `bench/fixtures/README.md`'s four HAL rows to the stale
`sha256`/fabricated-page-count values from `fe8e5d3` (still in `git log`) and
confirm the new test fails against them before writing the fix.

## Verification

- [ ] New/extended test fails against the reverted (stale) table
- [ ] New/extended test passes against the current, correct table
- [ ] `make check` green

## Invariants

- Must not touch `recipe.json` itself or the existing aggregate assertions.
- Must not require restructuring the table's markdown format.

## Exit criteria

Every row of the fixture README's record table is asserted against
`recipe.json`'s `sha256`/`page_count` for that row's `id`, so a future edit
that reintroduces stale per-row content fails `make check` the same way a
wrong aggregate count already does.
